      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Located on the right bank of the Middle Fork of the Forked Deer River, about 11 miles west of Trenton.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17,
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eaton%2C_Tennessee",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "location_description"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Eaton served as a vital shipping hub in the 1830s, with keelboats and flatboats moving goods to the Mississippi and New Orleans.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/eaton_tennessee",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "A post office opened in Eaton in 1830, with Dr. Lea as one of the first postmasters.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eaton%2C_Tennessee",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The community’s commercial peak in the 1830s included tobacco inspection by the Gibson County Court in 1837 and local furniture manufacturing.",
